Back at the office, Jim tries to revolutionize the office birthday party system and learns

53
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.270
that Michael's job is a lot harder than it looks.

54
00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:19.070
Oh, yeah.

55
00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:23.830
And for those of you really paying attention, Jenna just gave us a Dwight.

56
00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.170
Did I?

57
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:25.990
You did.

58
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.350
I Krasinski'd it.

59
00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:28.470
You pulled a Krasinski.

60
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:34.090
Fast fact number one, this episode was written by our very own Steve Carell.

61
00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:36.860
Applause.

62
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.460
We love you, Steve.

63
00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:38.980
Love you.

64
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.180
I reached out because I wanted to hear a little bit about the inspiration for this episode.

65
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:45.940
Here's what Steve told me.

66
00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:52.440
He said that he and his wife, Nancy, loved this show called Survivor Man.

67
00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.860
So Steve pitched the idea for this episode because he and Nancy were such fans of the

68
00:02:57.860 --> 00:02:58.560
real show.

69
00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.180
What is Survivor Man?

70
00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.340
I've never seen the show.

71
00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.780
I mean, I'm guessing the guy's got to go survive somewhere.

72
00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.860
Oh, Angela, you're going to be so happy.

73
00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:06.660
What?

74
00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.600
Fast fact number two is all about the real Survivor Man show.

75
00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:12.780
Give it to me.

76
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:20.680
It was a Canadian TV show starring a real-life survival expert and filmmaker, Les Stroud.

77
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.760
In each episode, he would be dropped off in a remote location and he would have to survive

78
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.380
up to 10 days.

79
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:34.900
He brought little or no food, water, or equipment except for his camera equipment because he would

80
00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:35.820
film himself.

81
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.560
There was no camera crew.

82
00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.340
But would he bring like a cup?

83
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:40.720
Like how does he get water?

84
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:41.800
No, lady.

85
00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:42.720
No equipment.

86
00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.300
No utensil.

87
00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:45.100
Correct.

88
00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:46.340
Aye, aye, aye.

89
00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.460
Now, they did have safety precautions.

90
00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.040
Like, you know in this episode how Dwight is kind of watching Michael from afar?

91
00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:53.680
Yeah.

92
00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:00.360
There was a support team who monitored this guy from a distance just in case he got attacked

93
00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.680
by a wild animal or something.

94
00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.420
They were not going to leave him out there to die.

95
00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.340
I would not be good at that job.

96
00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.760
At watching the man or being the man?

97
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:08.840
Watching the man.

98
00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:12.320
Um, I can't, I can't, I'm like, you know, I've got to put in my two cents.

99
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:13.940
How would that work?

100
00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:14.580
Explain.

101
00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.200
Well, like maybe he starts to go for a bush or something he shouldn't, you know, be touching.

102
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.280
Maybe it's the poisonous bush.

103
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.100
I don't know.

104
00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.600
I'd start going like, uh-uh, uh-uh, uh-uh, uh-uh, uh-uh.

105
00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.460
Or I'd pretend to be a bird, but like it would be like, no, no.

106
00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:39.790
Can you watch someone struggle?

107
00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:40.670
I'd be horrible at that.

108
00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:42.210
Well, let me say this.

109
00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:48.430
He was allowed to scout and study these areas ahead of time with a team of local experts.

110
00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.290
So he would go in with a survival plan.

111
00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:54.190
He would know the local fauna and flora.

112
00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.470
He would know what things he should eat or shouldn't eat.

113
00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:03.810
Something that Steve said was that he and Nancy noticed that the guy was always doing things

114
00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:08.810
like Michael does, like turning a stick into a spear, like he's going to go spear an animal.

115
00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:13.550
But he never actually succeeded in actually killing an animal.

116
00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:19.370
So Steve and Nancy had a theory that he just really didn't want to kill any animals because

117
00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:27.070
he was a really nice guy because he never seemed to actually catch any living food to eat.

118
00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:29.870
He was the friendly survivor man.

119
00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:30.450
Yeah.

120
00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:36.890
Fast fact number three is a location breakdown coming at you thanks to fan question from

121
00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:43.330
Catalina Rizzi, Sydney Weaver, Jason Barre, Sophia Spear, and Kate Betteridge.

122
00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:46.510
Where did Steve and Rain film the forest scenes?

123
00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:49.570
I'm going to guess the Los Angeles National ForeSt.

124
00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:50.410
Hmm.

125
00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:51.450
I don't think so.

126
00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:52.210
Oh.

127
00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:59.690
I mean, unless the Los Angeles National Forest is at the 44-mile marker on the Los Angeles

128
00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:02.650
Crest Highway, because that's where we shot.

129
00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:06.950
These scenes were filmed at a picnic site called Charlton Flats.

130
00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:11.390
And we spent one day shooting all of the forest scenes.

131
00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:13.150
And that day was...

132
00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:15.250
Can I get a Kentopedia from you, Angela?

133
00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.470
Because Kentopedia gave me this information.

134
00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:19.330
Kenta, kenta, kentopedia!

135
00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:21.330
Thank you so much.

136
00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:22.010
You're welcome.

137
00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.590
Wednesday, October 17th, 2007.

138
00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.450
That's such a specific Kentopedia fact.

139
00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:29.390
I know.

140
00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:37.210
So it was October, and that is right when the chill starts to come over Los Angeles.

141
00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:42.310
So some of the stuff where Michael is like, it's so hot in the day and then it's cool

142
00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:46.130
at night, that's actually really accurate for us here in L.A.

143
00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:47.690
That is life out here, guys.

144
00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:51.550
It's like you leave and you're like hot and then you're freezing in like four hours.

145
00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:52.490
You gots to layer.

146
00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:55.110
Yeah, it's a layering city for sure.

147
00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:55.510
Yeah.

148
00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.770
Of course, our show was set in Scranton, Pennsylvania.

149
00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:05.050
So it would have been much colder than we depicted at that time in Scranton.

150
00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:05.890
For sure.

151
00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:07.750
We didn't get the weather right, guys.

152
00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:08.790
We got it wrong.

153
00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:14.150
We portrayed a nice October day in Southern California.

154
00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:16.170
That's all I got, lady.

155
00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:20.970
Well, this whole episode reminded me of Creed Braddon because, you know, Creed's my neighbor.

156
00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.810
He loves the show alone.

157
00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.550
It's like a survivalist show, and he got Josh and I into it.

158
00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:31.970
And all I can think about when I watch these shows is, A, I would never make it.

159
00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:35.990
Not because I probably couldn't hunt and fish, but because I get too cold.

160
00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:37.390
What would do you in?

161
00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:38.690
I would get too cold.

162
00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.110
I think the lack of sleep.

163
00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:47.910
Whenever I watch shows like Survivor or like people doing extreme camping or survivalist

164
00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:53.250
type storylines, I always think about how they're not getting a sound night's sleep.

165
00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:55.670
And I really need that sleep.

166
00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:59.910
I am super cranky, but also I get physical symptoms when I don't sleep enough.

167
00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.030
I get a stomach ache.

168
00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:01.770
I get a headache.

169
00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:03.570
I get like real loopy.

170
00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:06.630
So I think it would be the lack of sleep that would do me in.

171
00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.450
I can sleep pretty much anywhere.

172
00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:13.930
I think I would figure out sleep, but I would just freeze to death or I'd get so cold.

173
00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:16.290
I would get angry and then I'd quit.

174
00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.450
You're like, I am out of here.

175
00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.870
This is too effing cold.

176
00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:24.390
I'm out.

177
00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:25.790
Screw you guys.

178
00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:27.070
Where's my heater?

179
00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:33.010
Well, my breakfast is kicking in, Ange, but I say we take a break and we'll come back to

